# Hosts a URL in this repository is allowed to name.
# See scripts/portability-check.sh. One host per line; '#' comments; blank lines
# ignored. Matching is exact and case-insensitive — a subdomain is NOT implied.
#
# ⚠ AN ALLOWLIST, NOT A DENYLIST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. The thing this
# guards against is a URL naming infrastructure the operator personally hosts,
# and nobody can enumerate those in advance — least of all in a file that ships
# with the repository, where writing them down would itself be the leak. What
# CAN be enumerated is the small set of hosts this project has a reason to name.
# So every new external host is a deliberate line in this file rather than a
# thing that slipped past a list of forbidden words.
#
# Reserved and unroutable forms are accepted structurally and need no entry
# here: *.example / *.test / *.invalid / *.localhost (RFC 2606, RFC 6761),
# example.com|net|org|edu and their subdomains, and any single-label host
# (docker-compose service names like `postgres` or `crypto` — a name with no
# dot cannot be a public domain).

# Project + licence
github.com
# Go module infrastructure. The toolchain fetches from proxy.golang.org and
# verifies every module against sum.golang.org on each `go install`, so these
# are named in scripts/go-install-retry.sh, whose whole reason for existing is
# that they intermittently fail. Public services this project depends on to
# build at all — not anything the operator hosts.
sum.golang.org
proxy.golang.org
www.gnu.org
fsf.org
keepachangelog.com
docs.renovatebot.com

# Specifications and prior art this code cites
jmap.io
www.jwz.org

# Third-party endpoints and fixtures that must be the real thing to be worth
# testing: the FCM push endpoint the web-push client actually talks to.
fcm.googleapis.com

# The Autocrypt Level 1 specification, cited in internal/autocrypt's package doc
# (#48). A public standards document, not anything the operator hosts.
autocrypt.org

# IP literals, all deliberate test fixtures. NOT blanket-allowed by range: a
# public address is exactly the shape a leaked VPS would take, so each one is
# listed.
127.0.0.1            # loopback
127.1.2.3            # loopback beyond .1: all of 127/8 is loopback (egress guard, MCP setup)
169.254.169.254      # cloud metadata endpoint — the egress guard MUST block it
93.184.216.34        # a stable public literal, for resolve-then-dial tests
10.0.0.1             # RFC 1918, egress fixtures
10.0.0.5

# Mail-provider OAuth (#52, ADR-046): the fixed endpoints and scope URLs of the
# two providers in internal/oauthmail's registry. Public services of Google and
# Microsoft, not anything the operator hosts. (IMAP/SMTP hosts are written
# without a scheme and need no entry.)
accounts.google.com
oauth2.googleapis.com
mail.google.com
login.microsoftonline.com
outlook.office.com
# Microsoft's legacy (v1) token issuer: named only in a test that proves an ID
# token carrying it is REFUSED (internal/oauthmail/connect_test.go).
sts.windows.net
# The vendored go-imap fork (#52, ADR-047; third_party/go-imap): public
# reference links in the upstream library's own README and comments, kept
# verbatim so the copy stays diffable against upstream.
pkg.go.dev
web.libera.chat
www-archive.mozilla.org
www.iana.org
www.rfc-editor.org
# Operator guide docs/oauth-providers.md (#52): the consoles where the operator registers their OAuth apps.
console.cloud.google.com
entra.microsoft.com

# GitHub Container Registry: where the release image is published.
ghcr.io
